 "rules-body": "\n    <h2>Rules</h2>\n    <h3>Timeline</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Every player starts with a starting card – just a year (no song) – on their timeline.</li>\n      <li>You hear a clip of a song (no title, no year) and decide where it fits on your timeline – before, between or after your cards.</li>\n      <li>Multiplayer: other players can make a <b>challenge</b> (1 token). In the “another spot” variant the challenger points to a different spot on your timeline – if you're wrong and they're right, they take the card. In the “exact year” variant the challenger names the release year – if it's exactly right, they take the card no matter what you did.</li>\n      <li>Naming the artist and title earns you a token (max. 5). 1 token = skip a song (the game shows what it was), 3 tokens = buy a card: before the reveal the song that is playing goes onto your timeline, after the reveal – a random card from the deck.</li>\n      <li>The first to collect the agreed number of cards wins. In single-player mode you have a limited number of mistakes; in the endless game you play until you run out of them and try to beat your record.</li>\n      <li><b>Expert mode</b>: besides the spot on the timeline you must give the selected details – title, artist and/or exact year. Otherwise the card is lost.</li>\n      <li><b>Teams</b>: each team shares one timeline and its members answer in turn. Otherwise the rules are the same as in multiplayer.</li>\n      <li>You can drag the card onto the timeline with your finger or mouse, or click a spot marked “＋”.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Name that tune</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Players take turns listening to a clip and typing the title, the artist or both (depending on the settings) – in one field; from 3 letters on, suggestions from all songs in the database appear.</li>\n      <li>Each correct part scores a point. Small typos, letter case and Polish characters don't matter.</li>\n      <li>For duets and songs by several artists, naming one of them is enough.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Against the clock</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>You have the chosen time limit (e.g. 2 minutes) to place as many cards as possible. The clock only runs while you listen and place a card.</li>\n      <li>A mistake doesn't end the game – the card is simply lost. The best scores go to a high-score table (separately for each time limit and level).</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Older or newer?</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>You start with a revealed card. Listen to the next song and decide: is it older or newer than the previous one?</li>\n      <li>The same year counts as correct. One mistake ends the streak – beat your record.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Decade duel</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>The game picks two decades (or you choose them). Every song is from one of them – say which. A correct answer = 1 point.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Who's first?</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Each player has their own on-screen button and key (1–8 by default; in the game settings everyone can assign any key). The first to press answers – the music stops.</li>\n      <li>Correct answer: +1 point. Wrong: −1 point, and the others can keep answering.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Single player and multiplayer</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Single player</b> – modes for one person (including the party).</li>\n      <li><b>Multiplayer</b> – first the player list: add people playing on this device or let them join by phone (QR code). Then choose the mode – players vote on their phones, the host decides. After the game the host chooses: the same mode and settings again, another mode, or the main menu (disconnects the phones).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>Party – minigames</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>A series of short minigames (choose them in the settings): Name that tune, Tune step by step, Decade duel, Older or newer?, Buzzer (2+ players) and Timeline (race to 4 cards, up to 8 players; for one person – one minute against the clock).</li>\n      <li>Several players: in each minigame 1st place gets 3 pts, 2nd – 2 pts, 3rd – 1 pt (ties score the same, a score of 0 gets nothing). In “Older or newer?” players answer in turn and a mistake doesn't end the game.</li>\n      <li>One player: minigame results (points, streak, placed cards) add up – beat your record.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Special cards</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Optional in the timeline game settings – about every fifth card is special: <b>double</b> (a correct placement earns an extra card), <b>token theft</b> (you take a token from the player with the most) or <b>timeline swap</b> (you may swap timelines with another player).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>Tune step by step</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li>First you hear 1 second of the song. You can guess right away or extend the clip to 2, 4 and 8 seconds.</li>\n      <li>Points for each guessed part: 4 (1 s), 3 (2 s), 2 (4 s), 1 (8 s). A wrong answer extends the clip.</li>\n      <li>A part you've guessed (e.g. just the title) is kept – you only guess the rest.</li>\n      <li>The first-letter hint costs 1 point.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>How songs are drawn</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Each artist appears at most once per game, and songs don't repeat in later games (in the same browser tab).</li>\n      <li>Themed sets (e.g. Eurovision, Opole, Polish Radio 3, film music) limit the draw to a chosen group of songs.</li>\n      <li>Difficulty: <b>Very easy</b> – about 1000 of the biggest hits; <b>Easy</b> – well-known songs; <b>Medium</b> – all songs; <b>Hard</b> – lesser-known songs, and on the timeline more songs released close to the years of your cards.</li>\n      <li>At every level songs come from different decades – no decade dominates.</li>\n      <li>With mixed songs the game keeps the chosen share of Polish and international songs.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>Big screen and stats</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li>📺 opens a second window for a TV or projector (drag it to the second screen and switch to full screen). This window then works as the remote.</li>\n      <li>📊 My stats: accuracy by decade and genre, most often missed songs, records and progress – stored only on this device.</li>\n      <li>The site collects anonymous visit statistics (Umami – no cookies and no personal data).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p class=\"muted\">Songs from the same year can be placed in any order. The year on a card is the year the recording was first released. Clips (30 s) come from the iTunes Search API.</p>\n",
 "{0}% polskich · {1}% zagranicznych": "{0}% Polish · {1}% international",
